.TH CCOL_SCOPED_PTR_MP 3 "2026-07-18" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_scoped_ptr_mp \- declare a raw pointer that a custom allocator frees automatically at scope exit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/common.h>

ccol_scoped_ptr_mp(name, type, mmgmt_procs);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares "type *name", sets it to NULL and registers an automatic
cleanup with __attribute__((cleanup(...))). You assign to name as usual,
with memory that mmgmt_procs->free accepts, which normally means
mmgmt_procs->malloc(size), or malloc(size) when mmgmt_procs is NULL.

At the end of the enclosing scope the value that name holds is freed
with mmgmt_procs->free, or with the default allocator if mmgmt_procs is
NULL.

This is the custom-allocator form of ccol_scoped_ptr(). That page covers
the default-allocator form and the warnings that apply to both macros,
about giving name a new value inside the scope and about releasing the
ownership with ccol_scoped_ptr_release().
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
name          The name of the pointer variable to declare
type          The type that the pointer points to (for example char,
              struct foo)
mmgmt_procs   The custom allocator (ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *) that frees
              the last value of name, or NULL for the default allocator
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Needs GCC or Clang, because it uses __attribute__((cleanup)).

The macro keeps a reference to mmgmt_procs instead of a copy, so
mmgmt_procs must stay valid for at least as long as the enclosing scope
of name. That always holds when mmgmt_procs is a variable already in
scope at the declaration, which is the common case.

The cleanup tests mmgmt_procs for NULL to choose between
mmgmt_procs->free and the default allocator. If you pass the address of
a local ccol_memmgmt_procs_t directly, the compiler can prove that the
test is always true, and GCC may then report -Waddress ("the address of
X will always evaluate as true"), which -Werror turns into an error. To
avoid it, put the address in a ccol_memmgmt_procs_t * variable and pass
that variable to both the macro and the allocation.

The macro expands to two declarations rather than one expression, so it
has to be a statement of its own, the same as every *_construct_scoped
macro of the library.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
There is no synchronisation. The pointer is a plain local variable and
follows the ordinary C rules for scope and thread-local storage. As with
every _mp API of the library, the thread safety of the functions in
mmgmt_procs is up to the caller.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
#include <stdlib.h>

void process(ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mp) {
  ccol_scoped_ptr_mp(buf, char, mp);
  buf = mp ? mp->malloc(128) : malloc(128);
  if (!buf) return;

  /* buf is used here */

}  /* mp frees buf on every return path */
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_scoped_ptr (3),
.BR ccol_scoped_ptr_release (3),
.BR ccollections (7)
